            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to hold dear, then the Latin for lovable."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ōsculum, ōsculī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ōsculum, ōsculī — \"a kiss\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Māter fīliō ōsculum dat. The mother gives her son a kiss."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Neuter, a diminutive of ōs — mouth: a little mouth. Ōsculor, already known, is to kiss."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ōsculum is a diminutive of ōs \"mouth\" (Lewis & Short; EtymDB). English osculate comes from the verb ōsculor."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
